  "prompt_text": "This schematic illustrates a molecular junction formed between two metallic electrodes, with a single molecule bridging the gap and its electrical behavior monitored by current and voltage measurements. The central message is that the molecule’s conductance can be actively tuned by several external stimuli. Around the junction, the figure highlights six control mechanisms: electrochemistry, current, electric field, mechanical force, pH, and light.\n\nEach stimulus appears to alter the molecular or interfacial state in a different way. Electrochemistry is depicted through added charges, suggesting oxidation or reduction. Current and electric field are shown as electronic influences acting along and around the junction. Mechanical force is represented by stretching or compressing the electrode spacing, implying a change in molecular conformation or contact geometry. The pH panel shows nearby H+ ions, indicating protonation effects. Light is illustrated as an incoming wave, implying photoinduced switching.\n\nThe left side shows the initial electrode–molecule–electrode setup, while the right side shows a modified molecular state, highlighted in green, after applying one of these stimuli. Overall, the figure conveys that single-molecule electronic devices can be reversibly controlled by chemical, electrical, mechanical, and optical inputs.",
